      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's what i was thinking. but it feels more active than just \"managing acoustics.\" like, if you look at the peace lilies specifically, their broad leaves are basically perfect parabolic dishes. if they were placed at specific angles, they could funnel sound from the desk area directly toward the center of the cluster. if there were tiny, low-profile microphones hidden in the soil or even integrated into the pots, the plants wouldn't just dampen the sound—they'd actually be optimizing the signal for capture. does the math for that kind of acoustic funneling actually hold up? like, could the leaf structure of a lily act as a natural amplifier for a sensor placed at the base?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
